>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research</p>
      <h1>Research and Provenance</h1>
      <p>
        Every record in The Dead Air Archive carries a history of its own. Some survive intact.
        Others exist only as copied notes, partial recordings, damaged photographs, secondhand
        accounts, or material reconstructed from several surviving sources.
      </p>
      <p>
        Provenance notes identify what is known about a record’s origin, what has been lost or
        changed, and where the surviving account becomes uncertain.
      </p>
      <h2>Reading a Provenance Note</h2>
      <p>
        A provenance note may identify the kind of source behind a record, whether names or
        locations have been altered, whether several accounts were combined, and which details
        remain disputed. It describes the limits of the surviving material rather than resolving
        them.
      </p>
      <h2>Archive Status</h2>
      <p><strong>Active.</strong> Material currently available in the archive.</p>
      <p><strong>Fragmentary.</strong> Only part of the record survives or can be reconstructed.</p>
      <p><strong>Sealed.</strong> The record exists, but access to its contents is restricted.</p>
      <p>
        <strong>Withheld.</strong> The archive acknowledges the material without reproducing it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Absence does not necessarily mean nothing was recorded. Sometimes it only means nothing
        usable remains.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
